## Smart pointer selection

- `std::unique_ptr<T>` — single owner, move-only. Default choice for any owning raw pointer.
- `std::shared_ptr<T>` — multiple owners with shared lifetime. Use only when ownership truly is shared; it has atomic refcount overhead.
- `std::weak_ptr<T>` — non-owning observer to a `shared_ptr`. Breaks reference cycles (e.g., parent/child graphs).
- Raw pointer / reference — non-owning access. Pass these into functions that don't take ownership.
- `std::make_unique<T>(...)` / `std::make_shared<T>(...)` — prefer over `new`. Exception-safe and (for `make_shared`) one allocation.

