```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

class Logger {
public:
    explicit Logger(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) {}
    void log(const char* msg) const {
        std::printf("[%s] %s\n", name_.c_str(), msg);
    }
private:
    std::string name_;   // RAII container, no manual new/delete
};

class Service {
public:
    explicit Service(std::unique_ptr<Logger> log) : log_(std::move(log)) {}
    void run() const { log_->log("running"); }
private:
    std::unique_ptr<Logger> log_;   // clear single ownership
};

int main() {
    auto svc = std::make_unique<Service>(std::make_unique<Logger>("svc"));
    svc->run();
    // Destructors run automatically, even on exceptions.
}
```

Key changes:
- Owning `char*` replaced with `std::string` — manual buffer management was a separate bug class entirely.
- `new Logger` / `delete log_` replaced with `std::unique_ptr<Logger>` plus `std::make_unique`.
- Constructor takes `unique_ptr` by value and moves it in, making ownership transfer explicit at the call site.
- `Service` no longer needs a destructor; the rule of zero applies.

## Common pitfalls I'll watch for in your code

- `shared_ptr` cycles (parent holds child, child holds parent) — fix with `weak_ptr` on the back-edge.
- Two `shared_ptr`s constructed from the same raw pointer — double free. Only convert to shared once, or use `enable_shared_from_this`.
- Arrays: use `std::unique_ptr<T[]>` or, better, `std::vector<T>`.
- Custom deleters for C APIs: `std::unique_ptr<FILE, decltype(&std::fclose)>`.
- Function parameters: pass `T*` or `T&` for non-owning access, not `shared_ptr<T> const&`.

