```typescript
type EventMap = Record<string, unknown>;
type Listener<T> = (payload: T) => void;

export class EventEmitter<Events extends EventMap> {
  private listeners: {
    [K in keyof Events]?: Set<Listener<Events[K]>>;
  } = {};

  /** Subscribe to an event. Returns an unsubscribe function. */
  on<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener: Listener<Events[K]>): () => void {
    let set = this.listeners[event];
    if (!set) {
      set = new Set();
      this.listeners[event] = set;
    }
    set.add(listener);
    return () => this.off(event, listener);
  }

  /** Subscribe once; auto-unsubscribes after the first emit. */
  once<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener: Listener<Events[K]>): () => void {
    const wrapper: Listener<Events[K]> = (payload) => {
      this.off(event, wrapper);
      listener(payload);
    };
    return this.on(event, wrapper);
  }

  /** Unsubscribe a specific listener, or all listeners for an event. */
  off<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener?: Listener<Events[K]>): void {
    const set = this.listeners[event];
    if (!set) return;
    if (listener) {
      set.delete(listener);
      if (set.size === 0) delete this.listeners[event];
    } else {
      delete this.listeners[event];
    }
  }

  /** Emit an event with a typed payload. */
  emit<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, payload: Events[K]): void {
    const set = this.listeners[event];
    if (!set) return;
    // Copy to allow listeners to modify the set during iteration.
    for (const listener of [...set]) {
      listener(payload);
    }
  }

  /** Remove all listeners across all events. */
  removeAllListeners(): void {
    this.listeners = {};
  }

  /** Number of listeners registered for an event. */
  listenerCount<K extends keyof Events>(event: K): number {
    return this.listeners[event]?.size ?? 0;
  }
}
```

### Usage

```typescript
type AppEvents = {
  'user:login':  { userId: string; at: Date };
  'user:logout': { userId: string };
  'error':       Error;
  'ping':        void;
};

const bus = new EventEmitter<AppEvents>();

// ✅ Payload is fully typed
bus.on('user:login', ({ userId, at }) => {
  console.log(`${userId} logged in at ${at.toISOString()}`);
});

const unsubscribe = bus.on('error', (err) => console.error(err.message));

bus.emit('user:login', { userId: 'u_42', at: new Date() });
bus.emit('ping', undefined);

unsubscribe(); // remove that single listener

// ❌ Compile-time errors:
// bus.emit('user:login', { userId: 42, at: new Date() }); // wrong type
// bus.emit('unknown', {});                                // unknown event
// bus.on('error', (e: string) => {});                     // wrong listener sig
```

### Design notes

- **`Events extends EventMap`** ties each event name to its payload type; `keyof Events` constrains every method to known names.
- **`Set<Listener<...>>`** gives O(1) add/remove and natural dedup of identical listener references.
- **`on` returns an unsubscribe function**, which is more ergonomic than tracking the listener reference yourself, and composes well with React `useEffect` cleanup.
- **Iteration over a copy** (`[...set]`) prevents mutation-during-iteration bugs when a listener calls `off` or `on` synchronously.
- **`void` payloads** require callers to pass `undefined` explicitly. If you'd rather allow `emit('ping')` with no second arg, overload `emit`:

```typescript
emit<K extends keyof Events>(
  ...args: Events[K] extends void ? [event: K] : [event: K, payload: Events[K]]
): void {
  const [event, payload] = args as [K, Events[K]];
  const set = this.listeners[event];
  if (!set) return;
  for (const listener of [...set]) listener(payload);
}
```

# Sequential vs Parallel Async/Await

## The Problem

The most common cause is `await`ing each call individually before starting the next one. Here's the buggy pattern:

```javascript
async function fetchAllData() {
  const users = await fetch('https://api.example.com/users').then(r => r.json());
  const posts = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts').then(r => r.json());
  const comments = await fetch('https://api.example.com/comments').then(r => r.json());

  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

### Why it's sequential

`await` pauses execution until the promise resolves. So the `posts` request doesn't even *start* until `users` completes, and `comments` waits for `posts`. Total time ≈ sum of all three requests.

## The Fix

Kick off all three requests first, then await them together using `Promise.all`:

```javascript
async function fetchAllData() {
  const [users, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('https://api.example.com/users').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('https://api.example.com/posts').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('https://api.example.com/comments').then(r => r.json()),
  ]);

  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

Now total time ≈ the slowest request, not the sum.

### Alternative: start promises, await later

Same effect, sometimes clearer when you need different handling per call:

```javascript
async function fetchAllData() {
  const usersPromise    = fetch('https://api.example.com/users').then(r => r.json());
  const postsPromise    = fetch('https://api.example.com/posts').then(r => r.json());
  const commentsPromise = fetch('https://api.example.com/comments').then(r => r.json());

  return {
    users:    await usersPromise,
    posts:    await postsPromise,
    comments: await commentsPromise,
  };
}
```

The key insight: a promise starts running the moment it's created, **not** when you `await` it. The bug is awaiting too early.

## Production-Ready Version

`Promise.all` rejects fast: one failure kills the whole thing. If you want partial results, use `Promise.allSettled`, and add HTTP-status checks since `fetch` doesn't throw on 4xx/5xx:

```javascript
async function fetchJson(url, options = {}) {
  const res = await fetch(url, options);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`${url} failed: ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

async function fetchAllData() {
  const results = await Promise.allSettled([
    fetchJson('https://api.example.com/users'),
    fetchJson('https://api.example.com/posts'),
    fetchJson('https://api.example.com/comments'),
  ]);

  const [users, posts, comments] = results.map(r =>
    r.status === 'fulfilled' ? r.value : null
  );

  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

## Quick Diagnostic Checklist

If you suspect sequential execution, check for any of these:
- `await` directly on a `fetch` call followed by another `await fetch` (the canonical bug)
- A `for ... of` or `for` loop with `await` inside (also serial; use `Promise.all(arr.map(...))`)
- `forEach` with async callbacks (doesn't actually wait, but also doesn't parallelize cleanly; switch to `map` + `Promise.all`)

